## The conflict of interest, stated plainly

This site is published by Age Ayurveda, which sells Ayurvedic products at
<a href={STORE}>ageayurveda.com</a>. A company publishing a reference work about the category
it sells into has an obvious incentive problem. Here is how we handle it.

- **Reference pages are not sales pages.** No product is mentioned in the body of a monograph.
- **One disclosed block.** Where a page describes something we sell, a single labelled block
  at the end of the page says so and links to the product. It is visually separated and it
  says who publishes the site.
- **No page exists because a product of ours needed one.** The monograph set was generated from a
  reference corpus covering the whole materia medica, not from our catalogue. Most pages have
  no commercial link at all.
- **Some pages compare named products, including other companies'.** A page may state what a
  named manufacturer publishes about a classical formulation and set it beside what the
  Ayurvedic Formulary of India specifies. Those pages exist because the comparison is a fact
  nobody publishes, and they follow five rules of their own, below.
- **No advertising, no sponsorship, no affiliate links.**

## Pages that name other companies

Until October 2026 this page promised that no page existed because a product needed one, and
said nothing about other companies' products, because there were none. That is no longer the
whole truth, so here is what replaces it.

We take no money from any manufacturer named on this site, including for a favourable
comparison, and we would not be able to accept it without saying so here. The conflict runs
the other way: we sell into the same category, so a comparison that flattered us would be
exactly the thing to distrust. The rules below exist to make that distrust testable rather
than asking for the benefit of the doubt.

- **The disclosure comes first.** A comparison page states, above anything else, that it is
  published by a company selling into the category it is comparing.
- **Organised by formulation, never brand against brand.** The unit of comparison is a
  formulary entry, not a company. There is no page ranking manufacturers.
- **Every figure is the manufacturer's own published figure**, quoted from their page, with the
  date it was read. A blank cell means that manufacturer does not publish that figure, and the
  page says so in those words rather than leaving the cell empty.
- **No score, no rank, no winner.** Publishing a composition is not the same as making a good
  medicine, and this site is in no position to judge the second.
- **The correction offer runs against us.** An error is corrected whoever it favours, and the
  page states where our own products come out worse. Corrections are logged in public at
  <a href={rel('/corrections/')}>/corrections/</a>.

A manufacturer who believes a figure here misreports their label should write to us. We keep
the page text we read and the date we read it, so a disagreement can be settled by looking
rather than by arguing, and if we were wrong the correction is logged under our name.

## How claims are worded

Ayurvedic products are regulated, and disease claims are a regulatory exposure rather than a
style preference. The rules we write to:

- In product blocks: "supports X function", or "traditionally used to support X". Never
  treats, cures, relieves or prevents. Never a disease name.
- In reference text: traditional indications are reported as the classical source states them,
  attributed to that source. Research findings are reported as the authors state them,
  attributed to the study. Neither is presented as advice, and neither appears next to a
  product link.
- Product names are never changed to make a claim easier to write. Some of our product names
  contain claim-like words. Renaming is a packaging and licensing decision, not a copy edit,
  so the name stays and the surrounding text does the work.

These rules are enforced mechanically, not by good intentions: the build fails if a disease
term appears inside a product block.

## What we will not publish

- Dosage recommendations for individuals, or anything that reads as a treatment plan.
- Manufacturing process detail. This is a reference for readers, not a formulation manual.
- Details of products that have not launched.
- Fabricated authorship or review credentials. See <a href={rel('/reviewers/')}>reviewers</a>
  for exactly where the Nighantu stands on expert review today.

## Safety framing

Every monograph carries a safety and contraindications section where the source material has
one, and every page carries a disclaimer. Where a substance is restricted, toxic, or a
metallic or mineral preparation requiring specialist handling, we have kept it out of the
public Nighantu entirely rather than publish a monograph a general reader might act on.

## Dates and revisions

Pages carry a last-reviewed date. Research sections state the year of the study. When we
correct something we say so on <a href={rel('/corrections/')}>corrections</a>.
